---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { loadSummary } from '../lib/summary';

const summary = loadSummary() as any;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const nf = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US');
---

<Base title="About" description="Meet the creator of Every Wonderful Dream and learn how the atlas uses federal land records to map San Bernardino County’s small tracts.">
  <header class="page-hero wrap about-hero">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>How the atlas <em>was made</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every Wonderful Dream takes its name from Wonder Valley, east of Twentynine Palms, where many
        dirt roads still follow the public land survey. The atlas traces that survey through the federal
        land records for San Bernardino County.
      </p>
      <p class="byline">Created by <strong><a href="https://www.sierraburkhart.com/">Sierra Burkhart</a></strong></p>
      <p class="creator-note">
        I own several adjacent tracts in Wonder Valley and have restored a homestead cabin on one of them,
        so the history documented here is as personal to me as the Morongo Basin sand that crept under my
        fingernails while I rebuilt the cabin. I earned my Ph.D. in geography at UCLA, where I built a
        graduate degree program and taught GIS and cartography. My work in web mapping informs how I bring
        the land records and survey grid together in this atlas.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="cabin-photo">
      <img
        src={`${base}/images/wonder-valley-cabin.webp`}
        width="960"
        height="1280"
        alt="Pale green cabin with white trim and desert mountains behind it"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
      <figcaption>My cabin in Wonder Valley.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </header>

  <section class="section wrap cols">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="About sections">
      <p class="label">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#sources">Sources</a></li>
        <li><a href="#method">Method</a></li>
        <li><a href="#limits">What is uncertain</a></li>
        <li><a href="#missing">What is missing</a></li>
        <li><a href="#corrections">Corrections</a></li>
        <li><a href="#credits">Credits</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The tracts</strong> come from the Bureau of Land Management’s Mineral &amp; Land Records System.
        The agency uses the legal descriptions in its case records to draw tract boundaries on the Public Land
        Survey grid. For San Bernardino County, the atlas includes {nf.format(summary.tracts)} patented tracts
        and {nf.format(summary.classifications)} cases shown as “classified land.” This release of the atlas
        data was generated on <time datetime={summary.generated}>{summary.generated}</time>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Section lines</strong> come from BLM’s national Public Land Survey System dataset (CadNSDI).
        <strong>Imagery and topographic maps</strong> are served live by the U.S. Geological Survey’s National Map,
        with aerial photography from the USDA National Agriculture Imagery Program at close range.
        <strong>Terrain</strong> is the open Mapzen terrain tile set hosted by Amazon Web Services.
        <strong>The county line</strong> is the Census Bureau’s. The federal data is in the public domain.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Names and dates</strong> come from BLM’s own index of California land patents, which the
        agency released under the Freedom of Information Act in 1997 and which Larry Vredenburgh has published
        ever since at <a href="https://vredenburgh.org/capatents/" rel="noopener">vredenburgh.org</a>. It lists
        each patent’s number, case serial, date and patentees. Joined to the map layer by patent number, it
        supplies a name and a date for {nf.format(summary.withNames)} of the {nf.format(summary.tracts)} tracts.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The patents themselves</strong> are at BLM’s
        <a href="https://glorecords.blm.gov/" rel="noopener">General Land Office Records</a>, the official
        archive. Each tract page links to its patent there, and where a name or date has been checked against
        the document the page says so.
      </p>

      <h2 id="method">Method</h2>
      <p>
        Records with the same land-office serial number are combined. The center of the mapped outline
        determines county inclusion, community assignment, and the point at which elevation is sampled.
        For regular subdivisions such as <span class="mono">E½ NW¼ SW¼ SE¼</span>, acreage is calculated from
        the legal description. For irregular tracts it is calculated from the mapped outline.
      </p>

      <h2 id="limits">What is uncertain</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Dates.</strong> Most dates come from BLM’s patent index. For the
        {' '}{nf.format(summary.tracts - summary.withDates)} tracts without an indexed date, the atlas estimates
        the year from the patent number and marks it “c.” Patents belonged to a single national number series
        until 1964. On records with known dates, the estimate matched the year 97 times in 100 and rarely
        missed by more than a year.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Names.</strong> The index is in capitals, surname first, and cuts every name off at thirty
        characters. The atlas turns <span class="mono">SEM RUTH</span> into Ruth Sem by rule, which will
        misplace the occasional two-word surname. Each tract page also shows the original index entry.
        Check the scanned patent if you need to confirm a name.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Outlines.</strong> BLM draws tract boundaries from legal descriptions. In some cases its map
        shows only the square-mile section. The atlas subdivides those sections where the description allows
        it, which gives outlines for {nf.format(summary.derived)} tracts. In a comparison with 12,000 five-acre
        tracts already mapped by BLM, half of the derived outlines came within about a yard of BLM’s and
        nine in ten came within 40 feet. The remaining {nf.format(summary.approximate)} tracts, mostly numbered
        lots, have records here but no individual outlines. Tract pages also flag cases where the mapped area
        differs substantially from the acreage in the description.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Completeness.</strong> The atlas holds what BLM has mapped. Leases that were never patented,
        relinquished claims and tracts whose cases were never entered in the modern system are not here.
      </p>

      <h2 id="missing">What is missing</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Patent images.</strong> BLM holds a scan of every patent. The atlas links to each one and will
        show the image on the tract page as they are gathered.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Applications without patents.</strong> By the national count, more than half of small-tract
        applicants never received a patent. The National Archives at Riverside holds the files for cancelled
        and relinquished entries from the Los Angeles land office.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What happened after the patent.</strong> A patent is the first page of a story. For later
        sales and parcel changes, start with the county recorder. Photographs and oral histories may tell
        you more about who built the cabins and who lived in them.
      </p>

      <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        When documenting a correction, cite the tract’s serial number and the source for the revised
        information. A patent scan or case file makes the correction easier to verify.
      </p>

      <h2 id="credits">Credits</h2>
      <p>
        Kim Stringfellow’s <em>Jackrabbit Homestead</em> documents the Small Tract Act across the Morongo
        Basin. Larry Vredenburgh published the California patent index that supplies most of the names and
        dates here. You can find both projects on the <a href={`${base}/resources/`}>Resources page</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Maps are drawn with MapLibre GL JS. The site uses BioRhyme, Petrona, Old Standard TT, Sometype Mono,
        Cutive Mono and UnifrakturMaguntia, all open-source.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .about-hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(12rem, 18rem);
    align-items: center;
    gap: clamp(1.75rem, 5vw, 4.5rem);
  }
  .byline {
    margin: 1.35rem 0 0.35rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  .byline strong {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .creator-note {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 36rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .cabin-photo {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .cabin-photo img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .cabin-photo figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.55rem;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 14rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .toc {
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(var(--header-h) + 2rem);
  }
  .toc ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
  }
  .toc a {
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .toc a:hover {
    color: var(--ochre);
  }
  .prose h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .about-hero {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .cabin-photo {
      width: min(100%, 17rem);
    }
    .cols {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .toc {
      position: static;
    }
  }
</style>
